{
  // Extends the workspace root, which is where the strictness lives: `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`,
  // `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `verbatimModuleSyntax`. The template shipped a `tsconfig.app.json` that sets
  // none of those — keeping it would have made this the one package in the repo where `undefined` goes
  // unchecked, and the linter said so out loud: four typed rules reported "requires the `strictNullChecks`
  // compiler option to be turned on".
  //
  // One file rather than the template's three. Its `tsconfig.json` was a solution-style stub for `tsc -b`
  // with references to an app and a node config; here the type-check is `tsc --noEmit -p packages/<pkg>`, the
  // same single pass `core` and `atlas` get, and nothing builds types.
  //
  // `lib` is restated rather than inherited, per the rule the monorepo split established: each package
  // declares the environment it compiles against, so a browser API used in a worker-only package is a compile
  // error rather than a convention. The Cartographer is a browser app — DOM, and a 2D canvas.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ESNext", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    // Required by React 19's automatic runtime: the transform injects the import, which is why the ESLint
    // config also switches `react/react-in-jsx-scope` off. Both halves have to agree or one of them lies.
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    // `vite/client` declares what a non-JS import means to TypeScript — `*.css`, `*.svg`, and
    // `import.meta.env`. It ships inside Vite, which is already a devDependency, so nothing is installed
    // for it; without it `import './shell.css'` is a type error even though Vite resolves it fine.
    //
    // Naming `types` at all REPLACES the default, which is "every @types/* package in scope". That is
    // safe here and checked: this package pulls React and Vitest through explicit imports, not ambient
    // declarations, so there was nothing being picked up implicitly to lose.
    "types": ["vite/client"],
    // The dev server's modules are imported by `vite.config.ts` with their `.ts` extension, which Node's own
    // loader requires — Vite 8 warns that it will load its config that way (2026-10-24). Allowed only because
    // nothing here is emitted by tsc (`noEmit`, from the root).
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true
  },
  // `src`, so the *config* files at the package root are left out — the same boundary `core` and `atlas` draw.
  // Test files are inside it and are checked, which is deliberate: a suite that no longer compiles is a suite
  // that stopped testing what it claims.
  //
  // `vitest.setup.ts` joins them because it is test code, not configuration: it calls `cleanup()`. Left out,
  // ESLint still reads it — it is a `.ts` file and `eslint .` finds it — but the project service cannot type
  // it, which fails the lint with a parsing error rather than a rule.
  "include": ["src", "vitest.setup.ts"]
}
